5493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554521 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554521 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186553864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554521 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d777c8fd-8f4e-5ca8-a5be-b50636868cd0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554524 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554524 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186554097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554524 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186554120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554524 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186554327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554524 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186554387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554524 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554524 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554524 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186553952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554524 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e485ffe4-c9b3-59e1-9725-0e402d6b690f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554531 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554531 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186554110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554531 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186554188 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554531 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186554363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554531 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186554400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554531 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554531 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554531 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186554188 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554531 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f1e8ec1f-41ee-5320-b7b5-0129ea48c897" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554556 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186554531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554556 #edb/ref 1069 "q-102805" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554556 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554556 #edb/ref 1071 "The function ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ is a solution to the equation $x{y}^{″}-(x+1){y}^{′}+y=0$ for $x>0.$ Use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554556 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2abfd6a6-feff-503c-bea4-5ba5b90fc5a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554556 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\cdot {e}^{x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u(x){e}^{x}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{x}+2{u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x({u}^{″}{e}^{x}+2{u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x})-(x+1)({u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x})+u{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({u}^{″}x+2{u}^{′}x+ux)-{e}^{x}{u}^{′}(x+1)-{e}^{x}u(x+1)+{e}^{x}u & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({u}^{″}x+2{u}^{′}x+ux-{u}^{′}(x+1)-u(x+1)+u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({u}^{″}x+{u}^{′}(2x-x-1)+u(x-x-1+1)) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({u}^{″}x+{u}^{′}(x-1)) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}\\ne 0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}x+{u}^{′}(x-1)=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}x+w(x-1)=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}x{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}x{e}^{-x}\\,⟹\\,u=-{c}_{1}(1+x){e}^{-x}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{e}^{x}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}(-{c}_{1}(1+x){e}^{-x}+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=(1+x).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554563 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186554524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554563 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75094" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554563 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554563 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -10 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{23}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554563 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "39a7d310-9046-5578-9f8d-31308eeefed9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554563 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -10 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-4,{u}_{23}=3,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{23}=-12.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554564 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186554502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554564 #edb/ref 1069 "q-33453" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554564 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554564 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(-2,-8,-1)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $2x+y-2z=-7.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554564 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "39c46748-4786-53ad-a218-6f5fdd2db8c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554564 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's start by picking a point on the plane. To do that, we simply substitute some concrete values for $x$ and $y$ into the equation and solve for $z.$ Let's choose $x=1$ and $y=-3$ (though it doesn't really matter what values we choose). Solving for $z,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+y-2z & =-7 \\\\ 2(1)+(-3)+7 & =2z \\\\ 6 & =2z \\\\ z & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(1,-3,3)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨1,-3,3⟩-⟨-2,-8,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,5,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane $ax+by+cz=d,$ the normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩.$ So, for the plane $Π,$ we have $n=⟨2,1,-2⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|3\\cdot 2+5\\cdot 1+4\\cdot (-2)|}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|6+5-8|}{\\sqrt{4+1+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\,3\\,|}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554582 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186554474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554582 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86452" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554582 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554582 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the function $f(x,y,z)={e}^{2x}-{y}^{2}z$, find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,{e}^{2}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554582 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6a99613e-1f33-5aa3-a707-361661349677" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554582 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})=(1,-1,{e}^{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2x}-{y}^{2}z)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2x}-{y}^{2}z)\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{2x}-{y}^{2}z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}\\text{ }i-2yz\\text{ }j-{y}^{2}k \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,-1,{e}^{2}) & =2{e}^{2}\\text{ }i-2(-1)({e}^{2})\\text{ }j-(-1)^{2}k \\\\  & =2{e}^{2}\\,i+2{e}^{2}\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{2},2{e}^{2},-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,{e}^{2})\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1,\\,z-{e}^{2}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨2{e}^{2},2{e}^{2},-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1,\\,z-{e}^{2}⟩ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554616 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186554482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554616 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37159" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554616 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554616 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554616 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "af173c80-5734-52f5-887a-4f2d2e37a60e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554616 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554672 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186554715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554672 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "48086a64-8671-5a00-bbb5-b58661fd7a59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554672 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186554623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554676 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186554730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554676 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "53334c82-cc45-5add-b5ff-772f81592394" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554676 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186554582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554677 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186554672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554677 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "588c4361-50c9-5029-a566-867e8863cbe8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554677 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186366808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554682 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186554749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554682 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7191b9bd-5ed6-54dc-8494-3debf9947402" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554682 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186394071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554715 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9ee2cdbe-40de-5a18-809a-b2ba9fbc04a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554715 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186554541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554716 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref